join detective right
detective: I suspect that you poisoned Dracula with garlic.
join cook left
cook (squinting): ...
detective: There was garlic in the hidden cabinet in the kitchen. Other than the gardener, you’re the only one who knows about that cabinet.
detective: You were in the kitchen practically all night. Both your account and the other witnesses’ accounts agree on that.
detective: Dracula collapsed when he started eating dessert.
detective: Vampires react pretty negatively to garlic, especially when they eat it.
detective: You know this, and yet you kept garlic in the kitchen.
detective: Therefore, I suspect you used this garlic to poison the Count.
cook (unamused): …
cook: You’re right. I put garlic in the Count’s dessert.
cook (speaking): But it wasn't my idea.
cook: [NIECE] told me to do it.
cook (squinting): Well, she paid me to do it.
cook (unamused): But she came up with the idea! Don’t ask me why she wanted me to poison him, because I don’t know. The money was enough that I didn't need to ask.
cook: She even smuggled the garlic over here. That’s why it’s in an airtight bag, so it wouldn’t affect her.
cook: Anyway, it’s not like we actually wanted to hurt him. I put only a tiny dose of garlic into the dessert, just enough to keep the Count down for what, ten minutes? Twenty? Maybe less.
cook (speaking): If I had to guess, if the Count hadn't been stabbed dead, he'd have already been awake by the same time we discovered the body.
cook: Basically, while we poisoned him, we’re not responsible for the Count’s death.
detective: Then how do you explain this stake I found in the kitchen?
cook: …?!
detective: It looks like a backup of the murder weapon to me.
cook (squinting): What? No, why was that in the kitchen?
cook: I didn’t put it anywhere in the kitchen! 
cook (unamused): And I definitely didn’t kill the Count! 
cook: …
cook (squinting): I must have been framed.

